course: biology
lesson: 119
title: How real cats hunt: stalking, waiting and pouncing
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Watch a cat with a toy mouse: it crouches low, its pupils go huge and round, its bottom wiggles, and then it shoots forward in one quick pounce. That is a hunter's body doing what it was built for.
explain: Real cats mostly hunt by waiting, not chasing: a cat may sit perfectly still beside a hole in the garden for an hour, ears swivelling to catch the faintest scratch, because waiting saves energy and a still cat is hard to notice.
explain: Its tools are amazing: eyes with a shiny mirror layer that makes them glow green in a torch beam, ears that turn on their own to point at a sound, and whiskers that sweep forward around the prey at the moment of the catch.
explain: Kittens learn from their mother, who brings them prey to practise on, and even a pet cat that sleeps most of the day on the sofa may still catch a bird in the garden at dawn or dusk.
fact: Just before a pounce a cat wiggles its bottom to set its back feet evenly and test its grip, which gives the most power in one leap; a cat can jump about five or six times its own height.
fact: Cats often hunt by sitting and waiting near a place where prey will come out, which uses much less energy than running; pet cats often wait a long time and catch nothing.
fact: A cat's pupils open wide in dim light or when it is excited and shrink to thin slits in bright light; slit pupils, which small cats have, can close very tightly in sun and open very wide at dusk, while lions have round pupils.
fact: Cats can see in about one sixth of the light people need, but in total darkness a cat sees nothing, just like you.
fact: Behind a cat's retina (the back lining of the eye that senses light) is a shiny layer called the tapetum that bounces light back through the eye for a second chance, which causes the eye-shine; people have no tapetum, so our eyes do not shine green; road «cat's eyes» reflectors copy the idea.
fact: Each cat ear has about 30 muscles and can turn on its own to pinpoint a sound, and cats hear very high sounds, including mouse squeaks, far above what people hear.
fact: Whiskers are thick, deep-rooted hairs full of nerves; cats cannot focus well very close to their noses, so the whiskers sweep forward to feel the prey for the last few centimetres, and cats also have stiff whiskers on the backs of their front legs.
fact: Cats often chatter their teeth at a bird they cannot reach; scientists think it shows excitement or frustration, though no one is sure.
fact: Cats often bat prey about before killing it, which keeps their face away from its teeth and claws; well-fed cats play longer, and hungry cats get on with the kill.
fact: A mother cat teaches her kittens by bringing dead prey, then live prey, then hunting with them, and kittens play-fight and pounce on tails from about four weeks old as practice.
fact: Pet cats hunt birds, mice, voles, lizards and insects, and in places like Australia and on islands cats have badly harmed small wild animals; a bright collar or a bell and keeping cats in at dawn and dusk help birds.
fact: Cats sleep or doze about 12 to 16 hours a day to save energy for short bursts of hunting, and they are most active at dawn and dusk, when mice and many birds are on the move.
exercise: Why does a cat wiggle its bottom just before it pounces? | It sets its back feet evenly and tests its grip for a strong leap | It is doing a little dance to scare the prey out of its hiding place | It is shaking off dust from its fur | It is waving its tail to the other cats nearby
exercise: About how high can a cat jump from standing? | About five or six times its own height | About its own height, and never any more | About fifty times its own height | Cats cannot jump at all, they only climb
exercise: Why does a cat sit still by a hole for a long time? | Waiting uses less energy and a still cat is hard to notice | It has fallen asleep sitting up and simply forgot to lie down | It is guarding the hole for its owner | It is listening to music playing underground
exercise: When do a cat's pupils go big and round? | In dim light or when excited | Only when it is fast asleep in its bed | Only in bright summer sunshine | When it is chewing on some grass
exercise: Can a cat see in total darkness? | No, it needs some light, but much less than we do | Yes, perfectly, just as well as it can see in the daytime | Yes, using its long whiskers as torches | Yes, its eyes make their own light
exercise: What makes a cat's eyes shine in a torch beam? | A shiny layer called the tapetum behind the retina | A tiny light bulb that switches on deep inside the eyeball | Tears reflecting the moon | The cat being angry and hot behind the eyes
exercise: What road safety idea copied cats' eye-shine? | Reflectors in the road called cat's eyes | Traffic lights that turn green when a car comes near | Zebra crossings with black and white stripes | Speed bumps
exercise: Why can a cat's ears turn separately? | Each has about 30 muscles, so each can point at a sound | They are loose and flop around whenever the cat walks | It is a sign the cat is ill | Only one ear can hear at a time, so the other one rests
exercise: Which sounds can cats hear that people cannot? | Very high sounds, such as mouse squeaks | Only very low sounds, such as far-off thunder | No sounds at all | Only their owner's voice
exercise: Why do a cat's whiskers sweep forward at the catch? | It cannot focus well close to its nose, so it feels the prey instead | To tickle the prey until it laughs and gives up | To look bigger and much scarier to the mouse it is catching | Whiskers stop the cat from sneezing
exercise: Where else on a cat's body are there stiff whiskers? | On the backs of its front legs | On the tip of its tail | Inside its ears only | On its tummy
exercise: What do scientists think a cat's teeth-chattering at a bird means? | Excitement or frustration, though they are not sure | That the cat is cold and shivering | That the cat is hungry for fruit from the bird's tree | That the cat is singing to the bird
exercise: Why does a cat bat a mouse with its paw? | To keep its face away from the mouse's teeth and claws | Because it wants to make friends with the mouse and play together | Because cats cannot open their mouths | To clean the mouse before it eats it up
exercise: How does a mother cat teach her kittens to hunt? | She brings dead prey, then live prey, then hunts with them | She shows them videos of mice running about | She leaves them all alone to work everything out by themselves | She buys them toy mice only
exercise: What helps garden birds notice a cat in time? | A bright collar or a bell | A longer tail | Feeding the cat less | Painting the bird table
exercise: How long do cats sleep or doze each day? | About 12 to 16 hours | Only about 2 hours a day | About 24 hours, they never wake | About 6 hours, like adults
exercise: When are cats most active? | At dawn and dusk | At noon in full sun | Only in the middle of the night | Only when it rains
test: «my cat sits staring at a mousehole for ages and gets nothing, he's useless» What is right? | Sit-and-wait hunters often wait a long time and catch nothing, even wild cats | Real cats catch a mouse every time, so he must be ill or very lazy | Cats are not hunters at all | He is waiting for cheese to come out of the hole, not for a mouse
test: «cats can see in pitch black, that's why they're out at night» What is right? | They need some light, but only about a sixth of what we need | Yes, in total darkness they see perfectly, better than in daylight | No, cats are completely blind at night | Cats see only in bright colours at night, never in grey
test: Why don't your own eyes shine green in a torch beam? | People do not have the shiny tapetum layer behind the retina | Our eyes are too small to catch any torch light | Our eyes are always closed at night, even when we walk about | Torches only work on animals
test: How could a mouse's own squeaks help a cat find it? | Cats hear very high sounds and can turn their ears to the spot | Mouse squeaks smell of food, and cats follow the smell to the mouse | Squeaks make the mouse glow | Cats cannot hear squeaks at all, so they do not help
test: «gonna trim my cat's whiskers so they look neat» Why is that a bad idea? | Whiskers are full of nerves and help the cat feel its way and its prey | Whiskers grow back in one minute, so trimming is a waste of time | Whiskers keep the cat's head warm | Whiskers are what cats taste with, so it could not taste its food
test: Why would a well-fed cat play with a mouse longer than a hungry one? | It is in no hurry to eat, so it stays careful and plays | A full cat is too weak and sleepy to bite the mouse quickly | Hungry cats are scared of mice | Full cats cannot see properly, so they keep missing the mouse
test: A kitten grows up alone, never seeing its mother hunt. What would most likely happen? | It would usually be a worse hunter, since hunting is partly learnt | It would be the best hunter of all, as nobody got in its way | It would never chase anything at all in its life, not even a ball of string | It would learn to hunt from a book
test: In the Lion King, Nala pounces on Simba in play. What is that kind of play in real young cats? | Practice for hunting | A real fight to the end | A sign they are feeling ill | A dance to attract birds
test: «crap, the cat got a robin again» What could the family do to help birds? | Give it a bright collar or bell and keep it in at dawn and dusk | Feed the robins to the cat so it gets full and stops hunting | Let the cat out more at dusk | Nothing, cats never harm birds, the robin must have been ill
test: Why might cats and mice both be busiest at dawn and dusk? | Cats have evolved to hunt when mice are out | Mice like watching the sunrise | Cats are scared of the dark and the light | They agree to meet then
test: «my 3 cats are all staring at the bird outside, how many ears pointing at it, no 4 cats, Mum's one too» | 8, two each | 6, two ears each on 3 cats | 4, one ear each | 16, four ears each
test: «why do small hunters like cats have slit eyes but lions round ones» What is the best idea? | Slit pupils suit small animals hunting both in sunlight and at dusk | Lions are blind in daylight, so they need round eyes for the night | Slit eyes help cats eat grass | Round eyes are only for animals with big teeth and loud roars
exam: Identify the principal function of the tapetum lucidum in the feline eye. | It reflects light back through the retina, improving vision in dim light | It produces light of its own in conditions of total darkness | It protects the surface of the eye from dust, grit and other foreign bodies | It determines the colour of the iris
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the hunting strategy predominantly employed by the domestic cat? | It waits motionless near a location from which prey is expected to emerge | It pursues prey over great distances until the prey collapses from exhaustion | It hunts collectively in large organised groups | It obtains prey exclusively by scavenging the remains left by other predators
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning feline vision in the absence of light. | In total darkness the cat is unable to see, though it requires far less light than a human | The cat sees perfectly in total darkness, as well as by daylight | The cat perceives only the colour green at night, and every other colour vanishes until dawn | The cat relies upon its eyes to emit a faint illumination of their own in darkness
exam: Ascertain the approximate daily duration of sleep and rest in the domestic cat. | Between twelve and sixteen hours | Between one and two hours | Twenty-four hours without interruption | Between four and six hours
